  "text": "Order one oh one was placed in May twenty twenty-three, by a customer in Boston. Ten months later, she moved to Austin. Ask the warehouse today which city that order came from, and the answer depends on how her customer row handled the move. Dimensions change, and the model has to keep their history. That's this round: fifteen questions on history and the special dimensions. Nine get worked through, then six quick ones.\n\nQuestion one. How do you preserve history when a dimension attribute changes? With a slowly changing dimension, an SCD. Three classic types. Type one overwrites. Set her city to Austin, and both orders now say Austin, including the one placed in Boston. Its three hundred fifty dollars moved with her. Type two adds a row instead. Boston's version gets an end date, Austin gets a new row, and each order joins to the version true on its date: one oh one in Boston, one oh two in Austin. Type three keeps a previous value column: Austin, and before it Boston. That's one step of history. Move her again, to Denver, and Boston is gone. Type two is the standard whenever a report has to stay true to its dates. Type one still has a job: fixing a typo, where the old value was never true.",
